Thought for 5s

The bug is that registration is only happening in OnStartUp(). After a MOOSDB reconnect, the app may lose its subscriptions, so OnConnectToServer() must re-register for all input variables.

Correct lifecycle pattern:

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  // Read mission-file parameters here.
  // Then register for mail.
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  // Re-register every time the app connects or reconnects to the MOOSDB.
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

void MyApp::registerVariables()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
}

Or, in AppCasting-style apps, the pattern is usually:

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();

  // Read config params here.

  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

void MyApp::registerVariables()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::RegisterVariables();
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
}

The key idea: OnStartUp() is for initial setup and mission-file parsing; OnConnectToServer() is where you must restore MOOSDB subscriptions after reconnects.

A common symptom of the wrong pattern is:

App receives NAV_X initially
MOOSDB or network connection restarts
App keeps running
NAV_X is still being published by another process
But this app no longer receives NAV_X mail

So for every variable consumed in OnNewMail(), such as NAV_X, NAV_Y, NAV_HEADING, NODE_REPORT, etc., put the registrations in one shared registerVariables() function and call it from both OnStartUp() and OnConnectToServer().

