gemini.text:
The issue you are running into comes down to how the MOOS architecture handles broker reconnections.

The Problem

When your MOOS app first boots up, OnStartUp() runs and successfully registers for NAV_X. However, if the connection to MOOSDB drops and reconnects, the MOOSDB server restarts its internal state for your client. It has no memory of what your app was subscribed to before the disconnect.

Because OnStartUp() only runs once when the process launches, your app never re-registers for the data after the reconnect.

The Fix: The MOOS Lifecycle Pattern

To fix this, you need to shift your registration logic into OnConnectToServer(). This method is explicitly designed by the MOOS framework to trigger every time a connection is established, including the initial connection and any subsequent reconnections.

Here is the corrected conceptual pattern:

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  // Do one-time initializations here (e.g., reading configuration parameters)
  return true;
}

bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  // This runs on the initial connection AND every automatic reconnect.
  // Registering here ensures MOOSDB always knows what you want.
  Register("NAV_X", 0);

  return true;
}

Summary of Best Practices

OnStartUp(): Use exclusively for one-time setup tasks that don't depend on the server's state, like parsing the .moos configuration file or initializing local variables.

OnConnectToServer(): Use for all server-dependent actions, primarily variable registrations (Register()).
